<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  AICOUNTLY Remote — the application manifest.

  Tauri embeds a default manifest that declares one thing: the Common Controls
  v6 dependency its dialog APIs need. This replaces it, keeps that dependency,
  and adds the two declarations a remote-control agent cannot be left without.

  dpiAwareness / dpiAware — PER MONITOR V2
  ----------------------------------------
  Without it the process is DPI *unaware*, and Windows virtualises every
  coordinate it reports: `GetSystemMetrics(SM_CXVIRTUALSCREEN)` and the
  rectangles from `EnumDisplayMonitors` come back in scaled, logical pixels
  rather than physical ones. On a 150% display that is the difference between
  a click landing where the viewer pointed and landing two thirds of the way
  there — and on a mixed-DPI pair of monitors, the difference between the
  right screen and the wrong one. It is the failure
  `src-tauri/src/platform/windows/input.rs` calls "the single most common way
  remote control lands clicks on the wrong monitor".

  Tao, the windowing layer under Tauri, calls `SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext`
  when it creates the event loop, so this was *nearly* true already. Nearly is
  not a property to rest a coordinate system on: a manifest is in force from
  the first instruction of the process, cannot be reordered by a change to
  when something else initialises, and is the way Microsoft documents it.

  requestedExecutionLevel — asInvoker
  -----------------------------------
  Stated rather than inferred. The application is not an installer and must
  never ask for elevation: it runs as whoever signed in, and everything that
  genuinely needs privilege is the service's. Declaring it also keeps UAC's
  installer-detection heuristics away from a binary whose name a future
  release might change.

  What is deliberately NOT here
  ----------------------------
  No `<compatibility>` / `supportedOS` block. That exists to make the frozen
  `GetVersionEx` report the real Windows version, and it has to be edited for
  every future Windows release. `platform/windows/device.rs` reads the version
  through `RtlGetVersion` instead, which is not subject to the shim, so there
  is nothing here to keep up to date.

  No `uiAccess`, no `highestAvailable`, no `requireAdministrator`.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity
        type="win32"
        name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
        version="6.0.0.0"
        processorArchitecture="*"
        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
        language="*"
      />
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>

  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <!-- Windows 10 1703 and later. -->
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <!-- The older spelling, for anything that reads it instead. -->
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/PM</dpiAware>
      <!-- Paths under %ProgramData% are short, but a redirected profile is not. -->
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
